.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt ZPOOL-REWRITE 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zpool-rewrite
.Nd rewrite the datasets and the metadata of a ZFS storage pool without modification
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zpool
.Cm rewrite
.Op Fl Av
.Ar pool
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Rewrite the blocks of every file system and volume of
.Ar pool ,
and the blocks of the metadata that the pool keeps for itself, as is without
modification at a new location, as if they were read and written back.
Each file system and volume is rewritten as
.Nm zfs Cm rewrite
of it by name rewrites it, see
.Xr zfs-rewrite 8 ,
then the metadata of the pool: the objects that describe its datasets, their
properties, snapshots and deadlists, the deduplication and block cloning
tables, the space maps of all vdevs, including those of a vdev that is being
emptied, the configuration, the history, the error logs and the other objects
of the pool, and the blocks that hold their dnodes.
.Bl -tag -width "-A"
.It Fl A
Rewrite only the blocks that have a copy on a top-level vdev whose
.Sy allocating
property is
.Sy off ,
see
.Xr vdevprops 7 ,
so that they move to vdevs that allocate.
.It Fl v
Print the progress of each dataset and of the metadata of the pool, what was
left alone and why, the number of snapshots of each dataset, and once done,
the space still allocated on each top-level vdev whose
.Sy allocating
property is
.Sy off .
.El
.Pp
The
.Fl A
flag helps to empty a top-level vdev, such as a special or dedup vdev, before
it is removed with
.Nm zpool Cm remove :
once its
.Sy allocating
property is set to
.Sy off ,
.Nm zpool Cm rewrite Fl A
moves off it every block that can be rewritten.
What remains is held by snapshots, which keep their blocks where they are until
they are destroyed, by datasets that cannot be rewritten, which are reported,
and by blocks that are being freed, which are gone after a few transaction
groups.
A file system or volume cannot be rewritten while its encryption key is not
loaded, while it is read-only or being received, or while its intent log has
not been replayed yet.
A deduplicated block stays where the entry of the deduplication table that
refers to it has it.
Intent log blocks are not rewritten: they are freed once the changes they log
are written.
.Pp
The metadata of the pool is rewritten in steps of about 16 MiB, each in a
transaction group of its own, and the datasets in steps as described in
.Xr zfs-rewrite 8 ,
so that interrupting the command stops it within a step; running it again
starts over, which with
.Fl A
skips what was moved already.
The command does not return before the rewritten blocks are on stable storage,
and the old ones freed.
It needs the same privilege as other pool configuration operations.
The pool cannot be rewritten while it has a checkpoint, which keeps the blocks
that are freed, while a vdev is being removed, or if it is not writable.
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 : No Emptying a special vdev before removing it
The following commands stop allocations on the special vdev
.Ar mirror-1
of the pool
.Ar tank ,
move every block that can be moved off it, and remove it once nothing is
allocated on it anymore.
.Bd -literal -compact -offset Ds
.No # Nm zpool Cm set Sy allocating Ns = Ns Sy off Ar tank mirror-1
.No # Nm zpool Cm rewrite Fl Av Ar tank
.No # Nm zpool Cm list Fl v Ar tank
.No # Nm zpool Cm remove Ar tank mirror-1
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vdevprops 7 ,
.Xr zfs-rewrite 8 ,
.Xr zpool-checkpoint 8 ,
.Xr zpool-remove 8
